{{Quote|There is only one way. The Way of the Mandalore.|Din Djarin, to Bo-Katan Kryze|<ref name="Chapter 11" />}}
The '''Way of the Mandalore''', also known as '''Way of the Mand'alor''', was a [[religion]] followed by orthodox [[Mandalorian]]s. It involved protecting fellow Mandalorians and taking in [[foundling]]s, but also wearing a helmet at all times. The [[Children of the Watch]] were a Mandalorian group, described by [[Bo-Katan Kryze]] as a cult, who followed the Way and sought to reestablish the religion, which had since fallen out of mainstream Mandalorian society and become considered ancient by the [[time]] of the [[New Republic Era]].

==Description==
===Overview===
{{Quote|I swear on my name and the names of the ancestors&hellip; That I shall walk the Way of the Mand'alor&hellip; And the words of the Creed shall be forever forged in my heart. This is the Way.|The Armorer and Ragnar recite the words of the Creed|<ref name="Chapter 17">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 17: The Apostate}}</ref>}}
[[File:Insider203previewtextless.png|thumb|left|250px|The Way of the Mandalore claimed that a true Mandalorian never removed their helmet in the presence of another sentient being, even within the presence of a loved one.]]
An ancient belief system<ref name="Insider 224">{{InsiderCite|224|A Certain Point of View}}</ref> practiced by orthodox members of [[Mandalorian]] society,<ref name="SW Book">''[[The Star Wars Book]]''</ref> such as the [[Children of the Watch]],<ref name="Chapter 11" /> the Way of the Mandalore consisted of a code of behavior and traditions that honored Mandalorian heritage.<ref name="SW Book" /> It involved protecting fellow Mandalorians but also wearing a [[helmet]] at all times. The Way stated that if a Mandalorian removed their helmet in front of another living being,<ref name="Chapter 8" /> they were no longer permitted to wear it<ref name="Chapter 4">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 4: Sanctuary}}</ref> nor considered Mandalorians anymore. Instead, they were considered [[Apostate (religion)|apostates]]<ref name="Return of the Mandalorian" /> and were issued with a [[decree of exile]]. Redemption was only possible through bathing within the [[Living Waters]] beneath the [[Mines of Mandalore]].<ref name="Chapter 17" />

It was only considered acceptable for a follower of the Way to not wear a helmet if they were too young to speak the Creed and to temporarily remove it in order to eat, and even then they had to do so out of the sight of others. When food was served, each Mandalorian would be responsible of finding a hiding place away from their company, except for war party leaders who had the honor of staying by the [[fire]] to keep warm.<ref name="Chapter 20">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 20: The Foundling}}</ref> Mandalorians who adhered to the Way would state "This is the Way,"<ref name="Chapter 3" /> a legendary Mandalorian motto,<ref name="Insider">{{InsiderCite|219|Launchpad}}</ref> when following its ideals.<ref name="Chapter 3" /> Mandalorians not of that [[Mandalorian Creed|Creed]] would treat followers of the Way with scorn,<ref name="Chapter 11" /> and viewed their beliefs as superstitious.<ref name="Chapter 17" /> In turn, the followers of the Way saw those individuals as not being true Mandalorians.<ref name="Chapter 11" /> The followers of the Way believed in curses that were prophesized in the Creed and claimed that their adherence to the Way preserved the Mandalorian legacy for generations.<ref name="Return of the Mandalorian" /> Adherents also saw keeping their word was a matter of honor.<ref name="Boba Chapter 7">{{BOBF|Chapter 7: In the Name of Honor}}</ref>

===Foundlings===
[[File:TheMediator-CH20.png|thumb|right|300px|Orphaned or lost children were taken into the Way as foundlings and then rose to become apprentices]]
Followers of the Way believed [[foundling]]s were the future and reserved [[beskar]] for them.<ref name="Chapter 3">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 3: The Sin}}</ref> They took care of foundlings until they were of age or reunited with their own kind.<ref name="Chapter 8" /> When foundlings who followed The Way came of age, they performed a ceremony for the foundling's swearing of the Creed. The ceremony included banners with clan [[signet]]s and drummers. The foundling had to stand in water, recite the words of the Creed and to swear to walk the Way. A helmet was then placed on the foundling's head, never to be removed again, and the tribe joined to say "This is the Way." The foundling then swore to never remove their helmet<ref name="Chapter 17" /> in the presence of other living beings<ref name="Chapter 8" /> from that moment on.<ref name="Chapter 17" /> Foundlings would train until<ref name="Chapter 20">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 20: The Foundling}}</ref> they were taken as a [[Mandalorian apprentice]] by a mentor.<ref name="Chapter 24">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 24: The Return}}</ref>

==History==
===The Tribe on Nevarro===
{{Quote|When one chooses to walk the Way of the Mandalore, you are both hunter and prey. How can one be a coward if one chooses this way of life?|The Armorer, to the Tribe|<ref name="Chapter 3" />}}
[[File:VizslaTheTribeReveal-Chapter3.png|thumb|left|300px|Members of the Tribe followed the Way]]
The Way was followed by at least one Mandalorian tribe, known simply as "[[the Tribe]]," which resided in an underground [[Nevarro Mandalorian covert|covert]] on [[Nevarro]] during the [[New Republic Era|era]] of the [[New Republic]].<ref name="Chapter 3" /> In [[9 ABY]],<ref name="Date">{{MandoDate}}</ref> when the Mandalorian [[bounty hunter]] [[Din Djarin]] got into an altercation with [[Paz Vizsla]] in the enclave after Vizsla had accused Djarin of being a coward for using [[Galactic Empire|Imperial]] beskar to create his [[Mandalorian armor|armor]], a Mandalorian [[The Armorer|armorer]] reminded them both of the Way of the Mandalore, stopping the fight. Later, the members of the Tribe protected Djarin from members of the [[Bounty Hunters' Guild]] when he broke the [[Bounty Hunter Code]] by [[Incident on Nevarro|rescuing]] [[Din Grogu|Grogu]]. Despite the fact that revealing themselves openly would necessitate relocating their covert to remain in hiding, the Tribe assisted Djarin as a fellow Mandalorian who followed the Way.<ref name="Chapter 3" /> Following the battle, [[Gideon's Imperial remnant]] found the tribe and [[Attack on the Tribe|attacked]] it, killing many of the Tribe's members.<ref name="Chapter 8">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 8: Redemption}}</ref>

When the [[farmer]] [[Omera]] asked Djarin if he ever removed his [[helmet]] publicly, he stated that he never did as he followed his way of life since he was rescued by the Mandalorians as a young boy.<ref name="Chapter 4" />

After being caught in the explosion of an [[E-Web heavy repeating blaster cannon]] during a [[clash on Nevarro]] against an Imperial holdout led by [[Moff]] [[Gideon]], Djarin was badly injured. While [[Carasynthia Dune]], [[Greef Karga]], and Grogu escaped into the [[Nevarro sewer]], [[IG-11]], an [[IG-series]] [[assassin droid]] who had been reprogrammed as a [[Nanny droid|nurse droid]], insisted that he remove Djarin's helmet, but was instead greeted by [[The Mandalorian's IB-94 blaster pistol|Din Djarin's blaster pistol]] as he aimed it at the droid to stop him from removing it. However, he lowered his blaster when IG-11 reminded him that he was lifeless and therefore Djarin would not be seen by a living being, which allowed the IG-series droid to remove Djarin's helmet and heal him with a [[bacta spray]]. After he was healed, they went to the covert and found the Armorer, who had remained to salvage what she could. She told Djarin that according to the Way, the foundling was in his care until he was reunited with his own kind. She forged him a signet, making him and Grogu a [[Clan Mudhorn|clan of two]], and granted him a [[jetpack]].<ref name="Chapter 8" />

===Reuniting Grogu with his kind===
{{Quote|You expect me to search the galaxy for the home of this creature and deliver it to a race of enemy sorcerers?''"<br />"''This is the Way.|Din Djarin and the Armorer|<ref name="Chapter 8" />}}
Djarin, quested to reunite Grogu with his own kind, looked for other Mandalorians, as he thought they were able to lead him to a [[Jedi]]. He found [[Bo-Katan Kryze]], [[Koska Reeves]], and [[Axe Woves]] on the [[moon]] of [[Trask]]. However, as the three Mandalorians took off their helmets, Djarin angrily dismissed them after realizing they did not follow the Way. Kryze told him that her armor had passed down for three generations, that she had been born on [[Mandalore]], and had fought in the [[Great Purge of Mandalore|Great Purge]]. To Djarin's astonishment, Kryze revealed that he was one of the [[Children of the Watch]], a cult of religious Mandalorian zealots that broke away from Mandalorian society to pursue the re-establishment of the ancient Way of the Mandalore. Not believing her to be a true Mandalorian, Djarin left the group. Kryze and the others eventually met up with Djarin again, saving him from group of [[Quarren]]s. Kryze insisted to buy Djarin a drink. Inside a [[Unidentified inn (Trask)|local inn]], she convinced Djarin to aid them on their mission to steal weapons shipments from an [[Imperial Gozanti-class cruiser|Imperial ''Gozanti''-class cruiser]] that belonged to Moff Gideon's Imperial Remnant.<ref name="Chapter 11" />

[[File:ByeByeBaby-TMCh16.png|thumb|right|250px|Djarin, without his helmet on, says goodbye to Grogu.]]
In exchange, Kryze promised to give Djarin information about the location of a Jedi. Together, they [[Assault on the Imperial Gozanti freighter|infiltrated]] the ship. When Kryze stated that they were coming up to hijack the ship, Djarin complained about Kryze changing their deal. Kryze taunted him by uttering the mantra "This is the Way," telling him that if he wanted her information about the Jedi, he would help her take the ship. Eventually, they succeeded in their mission. Djarin declined her offer to join their group, and Kryze revealed the location of [[Ahsoka Tano]].<ref name="Chapter 11" /> Later, while [[Mission to Morak|infiltrating]] the [[Morak Imperial refinery]] alongside [[Migs Mayfeld]] while disguised as a [[Juggernaut Pilot]]s, Djarin had to download data from a terminal. As it required a facial scan, the Mandalorian wrestled with the decision, but removed his helmet to allow the scan. Recovering the data allowed him to locate [[Gideon's light cruiser]], where Grogu was being held.<ref name="Chapter 15">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 15: The Believer}}</ref> Later, the Mandalorian removed his helmet again to let Grogu see his face before the foundling left with Jedi [[Luke Skywalker]].<ref name="Chapter 16">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 16: The Rescue}}</ref>

===Redemption===
Sometime later, Djarin sought out his tribe again, and traveled to the [[space station]] [[Glavis Ringworld]]. He found the [[Mandalorian covert|covert]] in the substrata. Djarin presented the [[Darksaber]] that he had captured from Moff Gideon, and the Armorer recounted the tale of the [[Night of a Thousand Tears]], which was part of the Great Purge, and told him that although the Mandalorian civilization had stood for [[9991 BBY|ten thousand years]], it was nearly wiped out by the Empire. She claimed it was a curse prophesized in the Creed, because those who were born on Mandalore lost their way, and only those who had embraced the Way escaped the curse, including their sect which was cloistered on the [[Concordia]]n moon. Later, Paz Vizsla challenged Djarin to a [[Fight for the Darksaber|duel]], as he wanted to wield the Darksaber, although the Armorer intervened before Djarin struck the killing blow. When the Armorer asked the pair if they had ever removed their helmets, Djarin admitted that he did. Vizsla called him an apostate and ordered him to leave, while Djarin asked for forgiveness from the Armorer, who told him the only way to atone was in the living waters beneath the mines of Mandalore. Djarin then departed from them.<ref name="Return of the Mandalorian" />

Later, having joined with [[Daimyo]] [[Boba Fett]] on [[Tatooine]], Djarin became involved in a [[War against the Pykes|conflict]] with the [[Pyke Syndicate]]. When offered the chance to escape, Djarin refused, telling Fett that he had made a promise, even though the latter thought that it was "[[List of phrases and slang|bantha fodder]]."<ref name="Boba Chapter 7" />

Sometime later, the Tribe relocated to another [[Mandalorian secret covert|covert]] on a desert astronomical object with lakes. There they were joined with more surviving members of the Children of the Watch. They held a ceremony for the foundling [[Ragnar Vizsla|Ragnar's]] swearing of the Mandalorian Creed. The Armorer recited the words and Ragnar repeated her. The ceremony was interrupted by a [[lake monster]] when Ragnar was swearing to never remove his helmet. The tribe failed to defeat the creature, until Djarin arrived in his [[Din Djarin's N-1 starfighter|N-1 starfighter]], striking the beast with cannon fire. Djarin was still not accepted in the Tribe due to his [[decree of exile]]<ref name="Chapter 17" /> but would redeem himself by [[Mission to the Living Waters|bathing in the living waters]], accidentally resulting in Kryze being "redeemed" as well due to her diving in to save him once he fell.<ref name="Chapter 19">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 19: The Convert}}</ref>

In the end, the Armorer would decide that Kryze could walk "both worlds:"<ref name="Chapter 21">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 21: The Pirate}}</ref> the orthodox<ref name="SW Book" /> path of the Way and<ref name="Chapter 21" /> the unorthodox<ref name="SW Book" /> path of her background, allowing Kryze to remove her helmet once more.<ref name="Chapter 21" /> The Mandalorians who followed the Way and those of Clan Kryze, who did not, agreed to come together for the [[Conflict on Mandalore|reconquest of Mandalore]], joining with other Mandalorian survivors of the Great Purge once they reached the planet.<ref name="Chapter 23">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 23: The Spies}}</ref> Under the leadership of Kryze and the Armorer together, the Mandalorians would reclaim the planet from [[Gideon's Imperial remnant]] and usher in a new era: Mandalorians who walked the Way and those who did not lived together in peace.<ref name="Chapter 24">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 24: The Return}}</ref>

==Behind the scenes==
The Way of the Mandalore was first described in the [[2019]] live-action television series ''[[Star Wars: The Mandalorian]]''.<ref name="Chapter 3" />

